== fuller page ==
Two rules do most of the work in C4's grammar, and both show in a single
line [two].

Space binds. A bracket right after a word, with no space, is an operator:
in as(VIRTUAL), as is applied to VIRTUAL, one group. A bracket with a space
before it is plain text, so as VIRTUAL is an operator and then a separate
word [binds]. Because as( never occurs in ordinary English, it can end a run
of unquoted prose exactly, with no quotes at all [prose]. Quoting and binding are
"two delimiters for one job", so quotes never carry the weight alone; a
language fenced only by quotes is one where "a stray quote changes meaning
silently" [quotes].

Case resolves. UPPERCASE words, such as VIRTUAL, TAXONOMY, HOST and TAG,
are looked up in the taxonomy. Lowercase words are the language's
own small words (as, from, toward, into) or the new name being made, taken
just as written [case]. One line can hold both: (TAG) at its head is looked
up, and tag inside "(for C4: tag)" is literal.

That is why the short form is the normal one. Mint a name in lowercase and
its other spellings come free; mint it in uppercase and you pay with a
list. "The long one is the bill for choosing the harder case"
[terse].

These rules were read off three of Corwin's own examples on 2026-08-10,
after his nudge to "pick-up the clues about significance of space", and
offered back for correction [found].

