== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Look at the little train on the shelf. Do you see the gap in it?
spoke: That gap is a space. It's small, but it matters.
narrator: With a gap, there are two. With no gap, they go as one.
pin: Now look down at the floor. Those two cars are hooked together. One train.
spoke: And the big letters? They go and look in the list. Can you see the path up to it?
pin: Small letters don't. They stay just as they are.
[fuller A]
narrator [two]: Two rules do most of the work in {C4|C four}'s grammar, and you can see both in a single line.
spoke [binds]: The first one: space binds. A bracket right after a word, with no space, is an operator. Put a space in front of it, and it's just text.
pin [prose]: That lets plain words run on without any quotes. The run ends where a bound bracket appears, because that never happens in ordinary English.
narrator [quotes]: So quotes never carry the weight alone. If they did, a stray quote could change the meaning, and nobody would notice.
spoke [case]: The second rule: case resolves. Big letters get looked up in the taxonomy. Small letters are the language's own little words, or the new name being made, just as written.
pin [terse]: That's why the short way is the normal way. Make a name in small letters, and its other spellings come free.
narrator [found]: These rules were read off three of Corwin's own examples, and handed back to him to correct. Now they're yours to try.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: This is a space. It is a small gap.
narrator: With a gap, there are two. With no gap, they go as one.
spoke: Pin, the top train has a hole in it.
pin: Not a hole, Spoke. A space. That makes it two things, not one.
narrator: Big letters go and look in the list. Small letters do not.
spoke: Off they go, up to your list.
[fuller B]
